---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';
import ReachMap from '../components/ReachMap.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import Split from '../components/Split.astro';
import Quote from '../components/Quote.astro';
import fence from '../assets/fence-review.jpg';
import endGrain from '../assets/redwood-end-grain.jpg';
import foliage from '../assets/redwood-foliage.png';
import Action from '../components/Action.astro';
import { COMPANY, PHONE_HREF } from '../data/company.ts';
import { PAGES } from '../data/site.ts';

const meta = PAGES[1]!;
---
<Base title={meta.title} description={meta.description}>
  <Field tone="forest" reveal={false}>
    <div class="hero">
      <h1>A redwood mill in Mendocino County.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Agwood Mill &amp; Lumber is a family-owned sawmill in {COMPANY.address.locality},
        supplying redwood wholesale to the Northern California trade.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Field>

  <Field tone="paper">
    <Split>
      <div>
        <h2>One species, run properly.</h2>
        <p class="prose gap">
          Redwood is the only timber we mill. That is a choice about attention
          rather than a limit on capacity: grading redwood consistently means
          knowing how this wood moves, splits and seasons, and that knowledge does
          not transfer from one species to the next.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          We sell to lumber retailers and distributors across Northern California.
          They resell what we grade, under their own name, to customers we never
          meet. That only works if what is on the sticker is what is in the pack.
        </p>

      </div>
      <!-- Material, not premises: a photograph of redwood claims nothing about
           whose yard it was taken in. It is never captioned as Agwood's stock. -->
      <Photo
        src={endGrain}
        alt="Close view of redwood end grain, showing tightly spaced growth rings."
        caption="Redwood end grain"
        widths={[400, 800]}
        sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 30rem"
      />
    </Split>
  </Field>

  <!-- The quotation and the photograph came from the same review, from the same
       person, with permission for both. Keeping them together is what makes the
       picture evidence rather than decoration: it is a fence somebody built with
       this mill's redwood, shown next to what they said about it. -->
  <Field tone="deep">
    <Split>
      <div>
        <Quote cite="Google review">
          These guys mill some nice redwood. Good, thick cut lumber, well graded.
        </Quote>
      </div>
      <!-- Agwood supplied the lumber; they did not build this fence. The caption
           says exactly that and must not drift into "our work" or "a project". -->
      <Photo
        src={fence}
        alt="A newly built redwood fence, boards running vertically between posts."
        caption="Fence built with Agwood redwood"
        width={685}
        widths={[400, 685]}
        sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 30rem"
      />
    </Split>
  </Field>

  <Field tone="paper">
    <!-- Same two-column split as the section above, so the picture-and-copy blocks
         on this page share one shape rather than each inventing their own.
         `flip` reverses them VISUALLY with CSS order; the heading stays first in
         the DOM so a screen reader still meets the section before its illustration,
         and the mobile stack keeps that order too. -->
    <Split flip>
      <div>
        <h2>Where it goes.</h2>
        <p class="prose gap">
          Ukiah sits in Mendocino County, inside a hundred miles of most of the
          Northern California lumber trade — close enough to the coast for the
          redwood, close enough to the valley for the yards that sell it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ReachMap />
    </Split>
  </Field>

  <Field tone="deep">
    <Split>
      <div>
    <h2>What the law requires.</h2>
    <!--
      Deliberately narrow, and it must stay that way. The client approved stating
      the Forest Practice Act as jurisdictional context. Its permitted scope is
      exactly that: a fact about California law. It may NOT be rewritten to describe
      Agwood's own sourcing or to imply a certification the company does not hold —
      we do not know where their logs come from. The second sentence is the safety
      valve and must not be deleted to tidy the paragraph.
    -->
    <p class="prose">
      Timber harvested on private and industrial land in California is governed by
      the California Forest Practice Act, which requires approved harvest plans
      covering water quality, erosion and replanting. Agwood holds no FSC or SFI
      certification and does not claim one.
    </p>
      </div>
      <!-- A single sprig on transparency: a botanical specimen, not a forest.
           A photograph of a stand of trees would imply Agwood owns timberland,
           which is exactly the claim this section is worded to avoid making. -->
      <Photo
        src={foliage}
        alt="A single sprig of coast redwood foliage."
        caption="Coast redwood foliage"
        width={840}
        widths={[420, 840]}
        sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 28rem"
      />
    </Split>
  </Field>

  <Field tone="paper">
    <h2>Talk to the mill.</h2>
    <p class="prose gap">The office answers the telephone Monday to Friday, seven to four.</p>
    <p class="cta"><Action href={PHONE_HREF}>Call the mill — {COMPANY.phone}</Action></p>
  </Field>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero h1 { max-width: 15ch; font-size: clamp(2.3rem, 6.2vw, 3.8rem); }
  .hero .lede { margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 42ch; }
  .gap { margin-bottom: var(--stack); }
  .cta { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .split h2 { max-width: 16ch; }

</style>
